<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 11/20</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Compute antiderivatives of common functions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Relate antiderivatives to differential equations and motion problems.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Antiderivatives</title>

<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    What might we mean by the antiderivative of a function?
</p>
<p component="a">
    What might we mean by the antiderivative of a function? Undo the differentiation; find a function whose derivative is the one that I care about. Then look at general antiderivatives and connect to mean value theorem.
</p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            How do general antiderivatives relate to each other? How could you determine which of them you actually want?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            How do general antiderivatives relate to each other? How could you determine which of them you actually want? They are all vertical shifts of each other. If you pick any one point on the curve, that fixes which curve you want.
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Find the general antiderivative of each of the following functions.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x) = x^4 + 1</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x) = e^x</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x) = x^n</m>, for any <m>n \neq -1</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Antidifferentiation Formulas</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                This means we can develop antidifferentiation formulas based on all of the differentiation formulas that we had previously. It's just all of them written backwards.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                This means we can develop antidifferentiation formulas based on all of the differentiation formulas that we had previously. It's just all of them written backwards. Antiderivative of powers, exponentials, trig functions, inverse trig functions.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Differential Equations</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                The idea of antidifferentiation ties directly to differential equations, which is the idea of solving an equation that involves a derivative. 
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                The idea of antidifferentiation ties directly to differential equations, which is the idea of solving an equation that involves a derivative. The normal context that we will have in this course is: given <m>f'(x)</m> and an initial condition, solve for <m>f</m>.
            </p>

            <example workspace="3in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - \cos(x) + 5e^x</m> and <m>f(0) = 2</m>, find the function <m>f(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </example>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Motion Problems</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                The same ideas apply to motion problems.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                The same ideas apply to motion problems. Now we can go back from acceleration and velocity to position.
            </p>

            <example workspace="3in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Assume that the acceleration of an object is a constant <m>-a</m>. If the object is thrown upward with initial velocity <m>v_0</m> from initial height <m>s_0</m>, find a formula for the height of the object as a function of time.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            
            </example>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>


</section>